new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8f945cb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/_docstrings/objects.Perc.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+By default, ``Perc`` computes quartiles and the endpoints:
+
+.. plot::
+
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+    from seaborn import load_dataset
+    penguins = load_dataset("penguins")
+    so.Plot(penguins, y="flipper_length_mm").add(so.Dots(), so.Perc())
+
+Set an integer to request a different number of evenly spaced levels, or pass
+specific levels directly:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+    so.Perc(k=3)
+    so.Perc(k=[10, 50, 90])
+
+Percentiles follow :func:`numpy.percentile` semantics. The default ``method``
+is ``"linear"``; NumPy also supports ``inverted_cdf``,
+``averaged_inverted_cdf``, ``closest_observation``,
+``interpolated_inverted_cdf``, ``hazen``, ``weibull``, ``median_unbiased``,
+``normal_unbiased``, ``lower``, ``higher``, ``midpoint``, and ``nearest``.
+The output has one row per percentile level, with the level in ``percentile``.
+Combine ``Perc`` with a range mark to draw a percentile interval:
+
+.. code:: python
+
+    so.Plot(penguins, y="flipper_length_mm").add(
+        so.Range(), so.Perc(k=[5, 95])
+    )
